==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|warm|ly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɔɹmli/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈwɔːmli/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-warmly.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|warm|ly}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|er|more}}

# In a manner that maintains [[warm]] temperature.
#: {{ux|en|Be sure to dress '''warmly''' today!}}
# In a [[warm]], friendly manner.
#* {{RQ:Doyle Lost World|passage=I said so - I said so '''warmly''', for I felt that the Professor was an ill-used man.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} With emotion, especially some [[anger]]; somewhat [[hotly]].
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|volume=I|chapter=XIV|page=166|passage=One evening in particular, about a week after Colonel Brandon left the country, his heart seemed more than usually open to every feeling of attachment to the objects around him; and on Mrs. Dashwood’s happening to mention her design of improving the cottage in the spring, he '''warmly''' opposed every alteration of a place which affection had established as perfect with him. “What!” he exclaimed⁠—“improve this dear cottage! No. That I will never consent to. Not a stone must be added to its walls, not an inch to its size, if my feelings are regarded.”}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|volume=I|chapter=XII|page=135|passage=Elinor then ventured to doubt the propriety of her receiving such a present from a man so little, or at least so lately known to her. This was too much. “You are mistaken, Elinor,” said she '''warmly''', “in supposing I know very little of Willoughby."}}

